{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016ZaFGT7kGEoJg5vVrxpqFq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://github.com/cncf/curriculum/blob/master/KCNA_Curriculum.pdf\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Cloud Native Computing Foundation (cncf/curriculum)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D1\", \"D2\", \"D3\", \"D4\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"kcna-competencies\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA) Exam Curriculum — CNCF publication (text extracted from KCNA_Curriculum.pdf, 3 pages)\n9\t\n10\t\"This document provides the curriculum outline of the Knowledge, Skills and Abilities that a Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA) can be expected to demonstrate.\"\n11\t\n12\t## KCNA Curriculum\n13\t- 44% – Kubernetes Fundamentals: Kubernetes Core Concepts; Administration; Scheduling; Containerization\n14\t- 28% – Container Orchestration: Networking; Security; Troubleshooting; Storage\n15\t- 16% – Cloud Native Application Delivery: Application Delivery; Debugging\n16\t- 12% – Cloud Native Architecture: Observability; Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles; Cloud Native Community and Collaboration\n17\t\n18\t(The PDF contains the typo \"Could Native Community and Collaboration\" for the last competency.)\n19\t\n20\t\"Cloud native computing uses an open source software stack to deploy applications as microservices, packaging each part into its own container, and dynamically orchestrating those containers to optimize resource utilization. The Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF) hosts critical components of those software stacks including Kubernetes, Fluentd, Linkerd, Prometheus, OpenTracing and gRPC; brings together the industry's top developers, end users, and vendors; and serves as a neutral home for collaboration. CNCF is part of The Linux Foundation, a nonprofit organization.\"\n21\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff5d535c-977f-479a-940c-05cafc9660f7","uuid":"5ea46a3b-8d9a-478f-9661-7b71d89bbac5","timestamp":"2026-08-25T06:43:18.800Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://github.com/cncf/curriculum/blob/master/KCNA_Curriculum.pdf\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Cloud Native Computing Foundation (cncf/curriculum)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D1\", \"D2\", \"D3\", \"D4\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"kcna-competencies\"]\n---\n# Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA) Exam Curriculum — CNCF publication (text extracted from KCNA_Curriculum.pdf, 3 pages)\n\n\"This document provides the curriculum outline of the Knowledge, Skills and Abilities that a Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate (KCNA) can be expected to demonstrate.\"\n\n## KCNA Curriculum\n- 44% – Kubernetes Fundamentals: Kubernetes Core Concepts; Administration; Scheduling; Containerization\n- 28% – Container Orchestration: Networking; Security; Troubleshooting; Storage\n- 16% – Cloud Native Application Delivery: Application Delivery; Debugging\n- 12% – Cloud Native Architecture: Observability; Cloud Native Ecosystem and Principles; Cloud Native Community and Collaboration\n\n(The PDF contains the typo \"Could Native Community and Collaboration\" for the last competency.)\n\n\"Cloud native computing uses an open source software stack to deploy applications as microservices, packaging each part into its own container, and dynamically orchestrating those containers to optimize resource utilization. The Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF) hosts critical components of those software stacks including Kubernetes, Fluentd, Linkerd, Prometheus, OpenTracing and gRPC; brings together the industry's top developers, end users, and vendors; and serves as a neutral home for collaboration. CNCF is part of The Linux Foundation, a nonprofit organization.\"\n","numLines":21,"startLine":1,"totalLines":21}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012seboDY3Y59oJ6zjPQgRBm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T23:15:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes.io/docs)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D1 Core Concepts\", \"D2 Storage\", \"D2 Networking\", \"D1 Containerization\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"extension-points\", \"cri\", \"cni\", \"csi\", \"device-plugins\", \"webhooks\", \"admission-control\", \"crd\", \"api-aggregation\", \"scheduler-extensions\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Extending Kubernetes (kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/)\n9\t\n10\tKubernetes is highly configurable and extensible. As a result, there is rarely a need to fork or submit patches to the Kubernetes project code. Configuration means changing flags, local configuration files, or API resources (such as ResourceQuota, NetworkPolicy and RBAC); extensions are software components that extend and deeply integrate with Kubernetes, adapting it to support new types and new kinds of hardware.\n11\t\n12\t## Extension patterns\n13\t- Controllers — client programs that read and/or write to the Kubernetes API, following a control loop: read an object's .spec, possibly do things, and then update the object's .status.\n14\t- Webhooks — Kubernetes makes synchronous HTTP requests to a remote service (a webhook backend); this adds a potential point of failure.\n15\t- Binary plugins — Kubernetes executes external binaries; used by the kubelet (CSI storage plugins, CNI network plugins) and by kubectl (plugins).\n16\t\n17\t## Extension points\n18\t1. kubectl plugins and client credential providers.\n19\t2. API access extensions — authentication, authorization, dynamic admission control (webhooks).\n20\t3. API extensions — Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs) and the API aggregation layer.\n21\t4. Scheduling extensions — scheduler plugins/profiles and custom schedulers.\n22\t5. Controllers — custom controllers managing custom or built-in resources; the operator pattern.\n23\t6. Infrastructure extensions — Device Plugins (custom hardware); Storage plugins (CSI, the Container Storage Interface, to add new storage types); Network plugins (CNI, the Container Network Interface, to implement pod networking); Container runtime (CRI, the Container Runtime Interface, to support alternative container runtimes); kubelet image credential provider plugins.\n24\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff5d535c-977f-479a-940c-05cafc9660f7","uuid":"b6c5019a-a7e9-45e5-bc99-8fc0d128517c","timestamp":"2026-08-25T06:43:18.801Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T23:15:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes.io/docs)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D1 Core Concepts\", \"D2 Storage\", \"D2 Networking\", \"D1 Containerization\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"extension-points\", \"cri\", \"cni\", \"csi\", \"device-plugins\", \"webhooks\", \"admission-control\", \"crd\", \"api-aggregation\", \"scheduler-extensions\"]\n---\n# Extending Kubernetes (kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/)\n\nKubernetes is highly configurable and extensible. As a result, there is rarely a need to fork or submit patches to the Kubernetes project code. Configuration means changing flags, local configuration files, or API resources (such as ResourceQuota, NetworkPolicy and RBAC); extensions are software components that extend and deeply integrate with Kubernetes, adapting it to support new types and new kinds of hardware.\n\n## Extension patterns\n- Controllers — client programs that read and/or write to the Kubernetes API, following a control loop: read an object's .spec, possibly do things, and then update the object's .status.\n- Webhooks — Kubernetes makes synchronous HTTP requests to a remote service (a webhook backend); this adds a potential point of failure.\n- Binary plugins — Kubernetes executes external binaries; used by the kubelet (CSI storage plugins, CNI network plugins) and by kubectl (plugins).\n\n## Extension points\n1. kubectl plugins and client credential providers.\n2. API access extensions — authentication, authorization, dynamic admission control (webhooks).\n3. API extensions — Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs) and the API aggregation layer.\n4. Scheduling extensions — scheduler plugins/profiles and custom schedulers.\n5. Controllers — custom controllers managing custom or built-in resources; the operator pattern.\n6. Infrastructure extensions — Device Plugins (custom hardware); Storage plugins (CSI, the Container Storage Interface, to add new storage types); Network plugins (CNI, the Container Network Interface, to implement pod networking); Container runtime (CRI, the Container Runtime Interface, to support alternative container runtimes); kubelet image credential provider plugins.\n","numLines":24,"startLine":1,"totalLines":24}}}
