{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015jGRsWr5hiRJqP9eRPfWkv","type":"tool_result","content":"# Container Storage Interface (CSI) – Opening & Objectives\n\n## Opening\n\nThe specification begins with author attributions and notational conventions referencing RFC 2119 for keyword interpretation (MUST, SHOULD, MAY, etc.). It establishes that implementations must satisfy all MUST, REQUIRED, and SHALL requirements to be compliant.\n\n## Objective\n\nThe primary aim is to \"define an industry standard 'Container Storage Interface' (CSI) that will enable storage vendors (SP) to develop a plugin once and have it work across a number of container orchestration (CO) systems.\"\n\n### Goals in MVP\n\nThe Container Storage Interface will:\n\n- Enable SP authors to write one CSI compliant Plugin that \"just works\" across all COs that implement CSI.\n- Define API (RPCs) enabling dynamic provisioning and deprovisioning of volumes, attaching/detaching volumes from nodes, mounting/unmounting volumes, consumption of block and mountable volumes, support for local storage providers, and creation/deletion of snapshots with volume provisioning from snapshots.\n- Define plugin protocol recommendations describing supervisor configuration of plugins and container deployment considerations.\n\n### Non-Goals in MVP\n\nThe specification explicitly will not define mechanisms for Plugin Supervisor lifecycle management, state maintenance, deployment/installation/upgrade/uninstall/monitoring, a first-class message structure for storage grades, protocol-level authentication and authorization, plugin packaging, or POSIX compliance guarantees. CSI \"SHALL NOT obstruct a Plugin Supervisor or CO from interacting with Plugin-managed volumes in a POSIX-compliant manner.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff5d535c-977f-479a-940c-05cafc9660f7","uuid":"cdbd9734-308b-43a2-b140-9b4504811e67","timestamp":"2026-08-25T06:45:17.887Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":202998,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Container Storage Interface (CSI) – Opening & Objectives\n\n## Opening\n\nThe specification begins with author attributions and notational conventions referencing RFC 2119 for keyword interpretation (MUST, SHOULD, MAY, etc.). It establishes that implementations must satisfy all MUST, REQUIRED, and SHALL requirements to be compliant.\n\n## Objective\n\nThe primary aim is to \"define an industry standard 'Container Storage Interface' (CSI) that will enable storage vendors (SP) to develop a plugin once and have it work across a number of container orchestration (CO) systems.\"\n\n### Goals in MVP\n\nThe Container Storage Interface will:\n\n- Enable SP authors to write one CSI compliant Plugin that \"just works\" across all COs that implement CSI.\n- Define API (RPCs) enabling dynamic provisioning and deprovisioning of volumes, attaching/detaching volumes from nodes, mounting/unmounting volumes, consumption of block and mountable volumes, support for local storage providers, and creation/deletion of snapshots with volume provisioning from snapshots.\n- Define plugin protocol recommendations describing supervisor configuration of plugins and container deployment considerations.\n\n### Non-Goals in MVP\n\nThe specification explicitly will not define mechanisms for Plugin Supervisor lifecycle management, state maintenance, deployment/installation/upgrade/uninstall/monitoring, a first-class message structure for storage grades, protocol-level authentication and authorization, plugin packaging, or POSIX compliance guarantees. CSI \"SHALL NOT obstruct a Plugin Supervisor or CO from interacting with Plugin-managed volumes in a POSIX-compliant manner.\"","durationMs":6545,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/container-storage-interface/spec/master/spec.md"}}
